# CASE 1 OVERVIEW
This case directory does not contain a .gitignore or .contextignore file, nor does it contain any
directories in the program's default ignore list. The subsequent tests assess the program's 
functionality under the most basic conditions:
- All files are human-readable
- All files, except for DESCRIPTION, have extensions that should be recognized as human-readable
- All files should be included by default

In addition, this case assesses basic logic, such as what gets printed to the console when the
program runs under various conditions, where the context.txt file actually gets generated, and
other fundamental attributes.

# TEST CASES
The following test cases should result in successful output, corresponding with the defined 
behavior for the program. 

## TEST CASE 1a
This test case should be run with one command line argument.

This is a basic test case that tests how the program handles:
- Directory tree building
- Various code file types (.py, .java, .cpp)
- Basic functionality of the program on a directory with subdirectories

A successful test result should display the entire contents of the directory case1 and all subdirectories,
except for this DESCRIPTION file.


**Command:**
```bash
get_context case1
```

**Console output:**
```bash
Generating context file for directory /Users/aiden/Code/personal/get_context/test_suite
Generated 'context.txt' at '/Users/aiden/Code/personal/get_context/test_suite/context.txt'
```

**context.txt output:**
```
Directory Structure:
case1/
	DESCRIPTION
	case1-0.txt
	case1-1.py
	case1_sub0/
		case1_sub0-0.txt
		case1_sub0-1.java
		case1_sub0_sub0/
			case1_sub0_sub0-0.cpp
			case1_sub0_sub0-1.txt
			case1_sub_0_sub0-2.py
	case1_sub1/
		case1_sub1-0.txt
		case1_sub1-1.js

==================================================

case1-1.py
print("This file does not contain a .contextignore or .gitignore")

==================================================

DESCRIPTION
Text not generated, file is not human-readable

==================================================

case1-0.txt
This file contains only human-readable files

==================================================

case1_sub0/case1_sub0-1.java

class case1_sub0_1 {
    String out = "Does this program output other programming languages by default?";
}

==================================================

case1_sub0/case1_sub0-0.txt
This is a textfile in a subirectory

==================================================

case1_sub0/case1_sub0_sub0/case1_sub0_sub0-1.txt
This is another text file.
By default, all contents of this directory should be added to context.txt.
There is no .contextignore or .gitignore, nor is there any file that matches the default exclusions.

==================================================

case1_sub0/case1_sub0_sub0/case1_sub_0_sub0-2.py
print("Does it read all .py files?")

==================================================

case1_sub0/case1_sub0_sub0/case1_sub0_sub0-0.cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Does this .cpp file get output in a nested subdirectory?" << std::endl;
}

==================================================

case1_sub1/case1_sub1-0.txt
This is a textfile in the second subdirectory with a depth of 1

==================================================

case1_sub1/case1_sub1-1.js
console.log("Screw it, does it work with JavaScript?");

==================================================
```


## TEST CASE 1b
This test should be run with .py listed as a command-line argument.

This test checks command-line argument logic. Specifically, it tests:
- does the program accept a second cl argument
- does the second argument work as anticipated

A successful test result should resemble **CASE 1a**, but only include text from Python files.

**Command:**
```bash
get_context case1 .py
```

**Console output:**
```bash
Generating context file for directory /Users/aiden/Code/personal/get_context/test_suite
Reading only files with extensions ['.py']
Generated 'context.txt' at '/Users/aiden/Code/personal/get_context/test_suite/context.txt'
```

**context.txt output:**
```
Directory Structure:
